  "narrative": {
    "intro": "RTX has $3.7B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. RTX won 658 awards so far, with an average award size of $5.7M. Obligations are down 48.5% year over year. The Department of Defense is the primary buyer, accounting for $3.7B in obligations. NASA and the Department of Homeland Security appear as much smaller buyers, with $164K and $1.7K respectively. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the largest market for RTX at $1.6B. Aircraft Manufacturing and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing each have $805M in obligations. Other Support Activities for Air Transportation accounts for $515M. R&D and small manufacturing and services markets also show activity, including $27.5M in Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences, and smaller totals such as $8.4M for Bolt, Nut, Screw, Rivet, and Washer Manufacturing. Current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity, and the largest-agreements tables show award-value figures as total contract values when present.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have RTX obligations changed this fiscal year?",
        "body": "RTX obligations are $3.7B in FY2026 to date. Obligations are down 48.5% year over year. Fiscal-year comparisons show prior-year totals of $7.3B in FY25 and $5B in FY24, with a peak of $8.2B in FY23. The current-year total is a partial fiscal year to date figure."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who buys from RTX and which markets matter most?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer, with $3.7B in obligations. NASA and the Department of Homeland Security follow with $164K and $1.7K respectively. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the largest market at $1.6B, followed by Aircraft Manufacturing and Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $805M each, and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $515M."
      }
    ]
  },
